WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.279
Okay guys, in this problem, a dump truck has an upfront cost of $100,000 and an expected

00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:11.560
salvage value of $15,000.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:17.160
It is expected to last 7 years before the truck falls apart.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:22.879
What is the closest to the assumed value of the truck at the end of its 5th year of service

00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:26.839
based on the straight line method of depreciation?

00:00:26.839 --> 00:00:31.539
So the first thing we are going to do here is go to our PDF reference manual and

00:00:31.539 --> 00:00:35.240
keyword search for depreciation.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:41.840
That will take us to page 38 or 37 of our reference manual and if you start on 37 you can

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.840
just scroll down to 38 and get the equation for the straight line method of depreciation

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.519
which is provided.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:01:00.520
So it is going to be dj equals c minus s sub n over n where dj is going to be the depreciation

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:12.340
value in your j.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.060
c is going to be our cost.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:29.100
S sub n is our salvage value in your n.

00:01:29.099 --> 00:01:45.299
And n equals the expected life of the asset.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:49.979
So we can start just plugging into the equation and filling it out.

00:01:49.979 --> 00:01:53.099
So dj is what we are solving for.

00:01:53.099 --> 00:01:56.179
dj is going to be $100,000.

00:01:56.179 --> 00:01:59.979
S sub n is going to be $15,000.

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:07.739
And n is the total lifespan of the truck.

00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:16.240
So solving for this we get $12,142 which is really dj but I prefer to think of it in this

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:38.800
problem as the rate of depreciation per year.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.879
So we can do one final step now to get our answer and find the value after 5 years of depreciation

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:46.960
of this asset.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.079
So we have got our initial value of the truck and we are going to subtract from that

00:02:52.079 --> 00:02:55.680
5 years of linear depreciation.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:06.719
We can think of this to match our units up as 5 years times 12,142 so that the units cancel

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.079
and we are left with a dollar to dollar comparison.

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:26.280
At the end result here would be $39,290 which we can see is very close to our answer of C.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.680
If the problem had wanted to try to ask the question in a slightly different way, they

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.599
could have asked what is the amount that the truck depreciated by at the end of its fifth

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:42.759
year of service, at which case we would just need to solve for one more basic step.

00:03:42.759 --> 00:03:47.199
So we now know the depreciated value at the end of 5 years.

00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:52.299
So we simply could have subtracted the original value of the truck or the depreciated value

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:56.919
of the truck from the original value of the truck and gotten the amount that the truck

00:03:56.919 --> 00:04:01.039
depreciated by at the end of 5 years.

00:04:01.039 --> 00:04:04.039
In this case the answer would have been about $60,000.
